A step runs on the database as the last deploy left it, before schema.sql's
changes: it cannot use a column schema.sql adds in the same release. Here
the step fills `slug`, which only schema.sql makes: refused, the database
unchanged. A dev database that took schema.sql's column first would have
run it: the rehearsal of production's says it before the deploy does.

The fix (deploy 2): the step adds the column itself. Nothing had run, so
the step could still be changed.

== before
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
INSERT INTO dish VALUES (1, 'Pea Soup');

== after
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, slug TEXT) STRICT;

== ledger
UPDATE dish SET slug = lower(replace(name, ' ', '-'));

== after
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, slug TEXT) STRICT;

== ledger
ALTER TABLE dish ADD COLUMN slug TEXT;
UPDATE dish SET slug = lower(replace(name, ' ', '-'));

== plan
-- after 1
roux: migrating test.db, ledger.sql's step 1
  step 1
    UPDATE dish SET slug = lower(replace(name, ' ', '-'));
-- after 2
roux: migrating test.db, ledger.sql's steps 1 to 2
  step 1
    ALTER TABLE dish ADD COLUMN slug TEXT;
  step 2
    UPDATE dish SET slug = lower(replace(name, ' ', '-'));

== error
-- after 1
migrating test.db: ledger.sql's step 1: no such column: slug

== query
SELECT id, slug FROM dish

== rows
1 | pea-soup
